B.yC=new A.aQ(B.yU,["Hello, engineer!","Hello, {n}!","Quick start","Appearance","System","Light","Dark","Scientists","Collect cogs and add famous scientists to your team. Your pick keeps you company while you solve.","Joined your team:","Team lead","Play engineering, live the formulas.","Courses","Start","Daily goal","{n} new questions unlocked!","Unlocks when {x} joins your team","Difficulty","{n} questions","Term card","Calculator","Use as answer","Engineering calculator","Arithmetic, powers, roots, \u03c0, brackets, trig (degrees), log and ln. Understands implied multiplication like 2\u03c0 or 3(4+1).","Basic","Easy","Medium","Upper-medium","Hard","Upper-hard","Very hard","Solve","How to use it, step by step","Which friction formula?","Common mistake","Where do the questions appear?","Every scientist on your team unlocks a 46-question pack. You meet those questions here:\n\n\u2022 The \u25b6 Solve button on the card: 10 questions from that pack\n\u2022 Play \u203a Daily questions: the 5 you have seen least from open packs\n\u2022 Play \u203a Bot duel: multiple-choice questions from open packs\n\u2022 Play \u203a Missions: story questions (locked ones show \ud83d\udd12)\n\u2022 Learn \u203a a formula \u203a Practice this\n\nDifficulty: Archimedes basic, Pascal easy, Torricelli medium, Newton upper-medium, Bernoulli hard, Euler upper-hard (estimation), Reynolds very hard.\n\nFormula count: Archimedes and Pascal questions use one formula, Torricelli and Newton 2\u20133, Bernoulli, Euler and Reynolds 4. Hard questions combine 2\u20133 topics (\ud83e\udde9 composite).\n\nTools \u203a Diagrams: solve questions with the Moody, pump-curve and viscosity charts.","Diagrams","Moody, pump curve, viscosity and flow regime. Solve questions on the chart.","Charts engineers use every day. Slide your finger across the chart and read the value.","{n} questions","{n} formulas","Composite","Formula chain","Diagram question","\ud83d\udcdd Solve with this diagram ({n})","How to read it","Tap the chart or slide your finger left and right.","\ud83d\udcc8 Open the diagram","Relative roughness \u03b5/D","smooth","Check with Colebrook","Pump curve","System curve","Operating point","Valve opening","Closing the valve steepens the system curve; the operating point slides left: less flow, more head.","water","air","Moody Chart","Friction factor f against Reynolds number and relative roughness (\u03b5/D).","Compute Re = V\xb7D/\u03bd and find it on the horizontal axis.","Take \u03b5 from the pipe material (e.g. cast iron 0.26 mm), compute \u03b5/D and pick that curve.","If Re < 2300, use the straight laminar line on the left (f = 64/Re), not a curve.","Read f to the left of where the curve meets your Re, then use it in Darcy\u2013Weisbach.","Pump and System Curve","How much can the pump deliver, how much does the system need? Where they cross is the operating point.","Pump curve (orange): the more flow, the less head the pump gives.","System curve (blue): static lift plus friction losses that grow with flow squared.","Where the curves cross is the flow and head the pump really runs at.","For power, put Q and H at the crossing into P = \u03c1\xb7g\xb7Q\xb7H/\u03b7.","Viscosity vs Temperature","How the kinematic viscosity of water and air changes with temperature.","Find the temperature on the horizontal axis.","The blue curve is water\u2019s \u03bd (left axis), the orange curve air\u2019s \u03bd (right axis).","Values are in mm\xb2/s: multiply by 10\u207b\u2076 for m\xb2/s.","Use this \u03bd in Re = V\xb7D/\u03bd to find the flow regime.","Flow Regime Map","Shows which friction formula to use for a given Re.","Re < 2300: laminar \u2192 f = 64/Re.","2300 \u2013 4000: transition, avoided in design.","Re > 4000, smooth pipe: Blasius. Rough pipe: Moody chart.","Curriculum","The 12 units and {n} formulas of a Fluid Mechanics course. Tap a unit to see its formulas.","Unit {n}","{f} formulas \xb7 {q} questions","My notes","New note","Note","Back to notes","Close","Delete","Jot down formulas, hints or solution steps here. You can reach them from every page.","Write your note\u2026 The first line becomes the title.","Saved automatically","Note deleted","Undo","Untitled note","Today","Learn engineering by playing","Measuring pressure","Computing flow","Checking Reynolds","Bernoulli ready","Tap to skip","Create your account","Appear on the leaderboard under your name. Your details stay on this device only.","First name","Last name","Email","Start","Enter your first name.","Enter your last name.","Enter a valid email (e.g. name@uni.edu).","Phone (optional)","Enter a valid phone number (e.g. +44 7700 900123) or leave it empty.","Welcome, {n}! \ud83c\udf93","Compete on the leaderboard by name","Your progress is saved","Your notes and badges stay with you","My account","Sign out","Sign out? Your progress stays on this device and continues when you sign in again.","Edit details","Save","Cancel","Exam Prep","Multi-stage problems of the kind set in university midterms and finals. Each exam has 4 problems.","Midterm Exams","Final Exams","Fluid properties, statics, buoyancy, rigid-body motion, Bernoulli, energy and momentum.","Dimensional analysis, pipe flow, pumps, cavitation, external flow, open channels and compressible flow.","Midterm {n}","Final {n}","4 problems \xb7 {p} parts","Best: {n}%","New","Watch an ad to enter","Exam ads left today: {n}/15","No exam ads left today. Come back tomorrow!","Enter the exam","Watch a short ad to open {e}. Afterwards the 4-problem exam opens.","\u25b6 Watch ad","Problems","Start the exam","Tip: each problem\u2019s parts (a), (b), (c) come in order; one part\u2019s result feeds the next. Calculator and hints are allowed.","Problem {p} ({x})","{n} parts","Suggested time: {n} min","Get ready for midterms and finals","10 practice exams \xb7 multi-stage problems","Fluid Mechanics and Thermodynamics are ready; more courses are on the way.","Not now","You can create your profile in Profile any time.","Create profile","Get on the leaderboard by name and keep your badges and progress.","Just a few details. Everything stays on this device and is never sent anywhere.","Update your details. Everything stays on this device.","Leaderboard","Badges","Progress","Create my profile","Notebook","Open your notes; brings the notebook back if hidden.","Hide notebook","Notebook hidden. Open it again with the \u201cNotebook\u201d button on a question.","Today\u2019s task","Start today\u2019s 5 questions","Keep going","Goal done! Keep practising if you like.","Solve your first questions; streak, accuracy and level will show here.","Background animation","Formulas drifting behind the pages","+{n} XP"],t.l)
